## Source and controls

- SQLite backup includes committed WAL data. The original WAL case passed;
  a new case holds an uncommitted write transaction while taking the snapshot
  through another connection. Restore contains only committed state and the
  original writer remains intact. Database and uploads are not a single atomic
  store: the caller must pause both writer classes, as documented.
- Exclusive creation refuses existing snapshot/recovery destinations. A
  canonical alias into the source namespace is rejected. All three journal
  sidecar spellings are refused before creating a restore directory.
- Extra, malformed, duplicate and wrong-kind inventory entries fail before
  output. Binary upload bytes survive exactly. The new synthetic inventory
  compares thirteen active/trashed account/tag/credential rows on IDs,
  junctions, type, trash, read rules, content, actors and dates before/after
  import and restore, including NULL, empty text and a NUL-bearing opaque
  synthetic credential. Neither real credentials nor private source were used.
- The synthetic opaque unsupported job handler and every delayed_jobs state
  column survive import and recovery unchanged. Parsing rejects the unsupported
  class; no worker is constructed and no job executes in this control. The
  original acceptance test's one synthetic job executes only into its local
  capture function, not a real delivery. Parsing retained jobs does not prove
  their real behavior or external effects.
- Original synthetic sign-in, PBKDF2 upgrade/rollback, session rollback,
  member/guest rules, bundled Markdown, attributed writes/history and failed
  target preservation pass independently. This does not prove original-user
  password possession or custom Ruby-mod compatibility.
- Importer mapping optimization resolves a table's fixed column indexes once.
  Existing NULL/alias precedence and schema-mapping controls pass, along with
  exact account/job preservation. Source SQLite is read-only; this review's
  inputs were isolated rather than live-changing production databases.

## Finding and surgical correction: issue 135

The recovery manifest was read before its file type was validated, while
root, upload entries and database already had link checks. The documented
refusal of links/special files therefore omitted the manifest path. Only
`tools/migrate/recovery.mjs` changes: lstat the manifest, require a regular
non-symlink file, then read JSON. No importer, engine, loader, host or name
implementation was changed by this review.

The first native file-symlink probe failed during setup with Windows EPERM;
it never reached restore. Its raw failure remains at
`.runtime/area5-file-symlink-probe-failed.xml`. It is now an explicit EPERM
skip, not evidence of native file-link refusal. Directory junction/alias
controls execute normally. A separate regular-file guard control reproduced
the old generic EISDIR error, then passed after the change with the explicit
manifest error before creating output. Its failing baseline is retained at
`.runtime/area5-manifest-guard-red.xml`. The symlink control remains portable
for a runtime able to create native file symlinks. No privilege setting or
installation was changed to make it run.

One evidence wording needs care: the original requirement table says corrupt
packages fail before output. Inventory/checksum failures do; an invalid SQLite
file accompanied by its matching checksum passes hash validation, creates a
diagnostic target, and then fails database validation. The new control checks
that failure and preserved original state. This agrees with the command docs'
partial-output policy, and does not justify claiming every corruption refuses
before output. A failed restore directory must never be selected for serving.

